---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Cash Flow vs Profit — Why They Diverge";
const description =
  "A company can report rising profit while running out of cash. How accrual " +
  "accounting creates the gap, what free cash flow measures, and how to spot trouble.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Fundamentals / Cash flow vs profit">
  <h1>Cash flow versus profit</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Profit is an opinion; cash is a fact. That line is a cliché among analysts
    because it keeps turning out to be true — most accounting blow-ups are visible
    in the cash flow statement years before they reach the headlines.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the gap comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Accrual accounting records revenue when it is <em>earned</em> and costs when
    they are <em>incurred</em>, regardless of when money moves. That is the right
    way to measure a period's economic activity, but it opens a gap between
    reported profit and the bank balance.
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>credit_sales</code><span class="meta">revenue without cash</span></div>
      <p>Goods shipped on 90-day terms count as revenue today. If the customer
        pays late, or never, the profit was still booked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>inventory_build</code><span class="meta">cash without cost</span></div>
      <p>Cash spent producing unsold goods does not appear in the P&amp;L — it sits
        on the balance sheet as inventory until sold. Cash leaves, profit does not
        move.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>depreciation</code><span class="meta">cost without cash</span></div>
      <p>The opposite case. Depreciation reduces profit but no money leaves in that
        period; the cash went out when the asset was bought.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>capitalised_costs</code><span class="meta">worth scrutiny</span></div>
      <p>Expenses recorded as assets instead of costs — some development spending,
        certain interest. Profit looks higher; cash is unaffected. Aggressive
        capitalisation is a classic way to flatter earnings.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The check that matters</h2>
  <div class="formula">Cumulative CFO over 5 years  vs  Cumulative PAT over 5 years</div>
  <p>
    Over a single year the two can diverge for perfectly ordinary reasons — a big
    order shipped in March, a one-off inventory build. Over five years, in a
    healthy business, cash from operations should be in the same neighbourhood as
    cumulative net profit, and usually somewhat higher because depreciation is
    added back.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>If five years of profit never turned into five years of cash, ask
    where it went.</strong> Usually the answer is on the balance sheet: swelling
    receivables, swelling inventory, or both.
  </p>

  <h2>Free cash flow</h2>
  <div class="formula">FCF = Cash from operations − Capital expenditure</div>
  <p>
    What is left after keeping the business running and funding its growth. This
    is the money genuinely available for dividends, buybacks, debt repayment, or
    accumulation.
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>negative_fcf_while_growing</code><span class="meta">not automatically bad</span></div>
      <p>A company building capacity will consume cash for years. The question is
        whether the spending eventually produces returns — check
        <a href="/learn/roe-roce/">ROCE</a> a few years later.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>negative_fcf_while_flat</code><span class="meta">bad</span></div>
      <p>Heavy capex with no revenue growth to show for it means the spending is
        maintenance, not expansion — the business needs continuous investment just
        to stand still.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Warning signs in the cash flow statement</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>cfo_below_pat_persistently</code><span class="meta">the main one</span></div>
      <p>Profit that never becomes cash. The most reliable single indicator of
        accounting trouble available to an outside investor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>receivables_outgrowing_revenue</code><span class="meta">deteriorating quality</span></div>
      <p>Revenue up 20%, receivables up 60%. Sales are being made to customers who
        are not paying, or on terms that keep loosening.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>financing_funds_operations</code><span class="meta">structural</span></div>
      <p>Negative CFO covered year after year by fresh borrowing. That can only
        continue while lenders keep lending.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>asset_sales_propping_up_cash</code><span class="meta">non-repeatable</span></div>
      <p>Positive investing cash flow in a supposedly growing business. Selling the
        furniture works once.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>A worked illustration</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    These figures are illustrative — not any real company — but the shape is one
    that recurs:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">Year    Revenue   PAT    CFO    Receivables
1       1,000     100    95     180
2       1,300     130    70     310
3       1,700     170    40     520
4       2,200     220   −10     820</div>
  <p>
    Revenue and profit compound beautifully. Cash from operations goes the other
    way, and receivables grow far faster than revenue — from 18% of sales to 37%.
    The company is booking sales it is not collecting. By year four it is funding
    its own operations from somewhere else, and the P&amp;L gives no hint of it.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>Why this matters more in India</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Working-capital cycles in Indian manufacturing and infrastructure are long,
    and receivable days of 90 to 180 are ordinary in several sectors. That makes
    the CFO-versus-PAT comparison more informative here than in economies with
    tighter payment norms — and it means the level matters less than the
    <em>trend</em>. Slowly lengthening collection is the signal.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/reading-financials/">reading a financial statement</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/debt-leverage/">debt and leverage</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/valuation-ratios/">valuation ratios</a>
  </p>
</Base>
